{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And he shall wash in water all his flesh.</b> You will not find among all the other impure persons the expression 'all his flesh' except in the case of the ba'al keri; and even in the case of the zav, whose impurity is more severe than his, Scripture did not mention 'all his flesh.' The reason is that man is composed of four elements, and although the arrangement of the elements in their spheres is ארמ\"ע (air, water, fire, earth), their arrangement in the creation of the world was not so — in their creation the air (רוח) was created first, then water, then fire." "<b>And so</b> you will find in the Book of Formation (Sefer Yetzirah): three mother letters אמ\"ש. The reason he called them 'mothers' is that they are mothers to all existing beings, and they are air, water, fire; and this is the order: water from air, fire from water, water from air. And this is what is written (Genesis 1:2): ״בהו״ — and darkness, this is fire from water; and ״ורוח אלהים מרחפת על פני המים״, this is water from air. Therefore we say in prayer: 'Who makes the wind blow and brings down the rain' — fire from water, for from water they bring forth fire; and from fire comes earth, which is the ash. And in man were created the white humor corresponding to water, the red humor corresponding to fire, the black humor corresponding to earth, and the element of air within him is the first, and it overpowers the other elements, and it is what produces a vessel for its action, for it is the principal." "<b>Know</b> that each and every element is itself composed of four elements. Since this is so, when the air (רוח) of desire grows strong in a man, all the parts of air latent within the other elements are roused — and even though the other elements are, at the time of sleep, as it were dead, the element of air that brought them into being is awake and neither slumbers nor sleeps, and it is what produces this effect, and it rouses all the parts of air concealed in potential within the other elements. Because of this, Scripture requires the ba'al keri to wash in water all his flesh — for the four elements are spread through all parts of the flesh, and besides the element of air which is first, in each of the others there is a portion of air. And this drop is not fit for seed and has no root in the generative power, because the other elements were not in it in mutual accord, for they were sleeping and dormant, and it contains only the power of air alone. And Scripture mentions immediately after: ״ואשה אשר ישכב איש אותה שכבת זרע״ — that is, that the four elements were in accord; it says ״ורחצו במים״ and did not say 'all his flesh,' because in it there was not the power of air alone as in the ba'al keri." "<b>And further</b> one may say: when it issues with intention, it is prepared to receive a living spirit (רוח חיים), because most of its parts are the natural air (רוח) which is the vessel of the soul, and the airy power prevails over it; and although it too is impure, Scripture did not mention 'all his flesh.' But in the case of the ba'al keri, which issues without intention, it resembles the flesh of a corpse — and this is the reason for the impurity of the ba'al keri, that Scripture mentioned 'all his flesh' regarding him. And the meaning of what Scripture said (Deuteronomy 23:11): ״כי יהיה בך איש אשר לא יהיה טהור מקרה לילה והיה לפנות ערב ירחץ במים״ — and it did not mention 'all his flesh' — is that ״ירחץ במים״ should be understood as was said in another place: 'all his flesh.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:38.152441625Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}